<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/advanced/strategy-competitive-advantage">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The intermediate marketing lessons taught you to segment, target and position, and to run the 4Ps. Strategy
    asks the question those tools cannot answer by themselves: <strong>why do some firms stay profitable for
    decades while their equally clever competitors bleed to death?</strong> The answer, in one line, is that
    profits are set by industry <em>structure</em> and defended by <em>position</em> — and both are visible
    before a single product is built. This lesson assembles the full apparatus: Michael Porter's five forces for
    reading an industry, the generic strategies for choosing a position, the moats that make a position durable,
    and Christensen's disruption theory for how apparently unassailable positions fall anyway.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="five forces, distinct positions, moats, and the power of saying no" widget="equationStep" config={stratCfg} />

  <h2>Reading the industry: the five forces</h2>
  <p>
    Porter's 1979 insight inverted the strategic conversation. Managers obsess over competitors; Porter showed
    that <strong>competition in the broad sense</strong> has five forces, and rivalry is only one of them:
    <strong>the threat of new entrants, the threat of substitutes, the bargaining power of buyers, the
    bargaining power of suppliers</strong>, and rivalry among incumbents. Each force presses on the price-cost
    margin from a different side. Buyers squeeze prices (or demand costly features); suppliers squeeze input
    costs; substitutes cap the price you can charge at all; entrants erode whatever margin survives.
  </p>
  <p>
    The classic contrast makes the framework bite. Compare US scheduled airlines with soft-drink concentrate
    producers over the decades. Airlines: aeroplanes can be leased, so entry is easy; the product is a
    commodity seat, so switching costs are zero and price-comparison sites arm every buyer; aircraft makers and
    pilot unions are concentrated suppliers; demand swings with the economy, so rivalry erupts into fare wars on
    every route. Soft-drink concentrate: two dominant brands whose rivalry, after early price wars, settled
    into non-price competition; customers loyal for life; franchised bottlers with little bargaining power. The
    result — airline economics hovering near break-even for the industry as a whole while concentrate earns
    spectacular returns — is not about effort, intelligence, or even management quality. Warren Buffett's
    running joke that investors should have shot down the Wright brothers at Kitty Hawk is the same point:
    a growing industry can be a terrible place to own capital, and structure tells you so in advance.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the biggest competitor is the biggest threat." Often the profit pool
  evaporates from a direction nobody was watching: substitutes. Video rental did not die because one rental
  chain outcompeted another; streaming changed the definition of the product. Five-forces analysis forces the
  wider scan — including the "good enough" substitute that undercuts on price rather than quality.</p>

  <h2>Knowing the customer: research and segmentation</h2>
  <p>
    Positioning begins with the customer, and the marketing syllabus's market-research topics are the evidence
    pipeline. Two skills matter at advanced level. First, <strong>segmentation is a strategic choice, not a
    demographic spreadsheet</strong>: segments are only real if they respond differently to the marketing mix
    (different price sensitivity, different channels, different needs). The useful questions are behavioural —
    who is served poorly today, who over-pays for attributes they do not value — not merely age and postcode.
    Second, <strong>research has a precision budget</strong>. At 95% confidence, a random sample's margin of
    error is roughly 1/√n: 400 respondents gives ±5 percentage points, 1,000 gives about ±3.2, and you must
    quadruple the sample merely to halve the error. A manager demanding "±1% certainty" on a new concept is
    ordering a 10,000-person study; the strategist's craft is deciding which questions deserve that money and
    which can be answered by 12 sharp customer interviews.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "just ask customers what they want." Customers are excellent at reporting
  current pains and unreliable at imagining behaviour changes; the familiar gap between what people say they
  want and what they actually buy is why serious research triangulates — stated preference, observed behaviour,
  and ultimately small test markets. Research reduces risk; it never certifies success.</p>

  <h2>Choosing a position: generic strategies and the marketing mix</h2>
  <p>
    Porter's generic strategies say there are only two coherent ways to beat the industry average across a
    broad market: <strong>cost leadership</strong> (acceptably good product at structurally lower cost) or
    <strong>differentiation</strong> (a product buyers will pay a premium for), plus a third option,
    <strong>focus</strong> — applying one of those two to a narrow segment the broad players underserve. Each
    position implies a complete, coherent marketing mix. A cost leader's 4Ps are not "the same marketing, done
    cheaper": distribution is high-volume and no-frills, promotion emphasizes price and availability, product
    range is deliberately narrow. A differentiator spends on brand, channels and service that a cost leader
    would regard as waste. The failure mode Porter named <em>stuck-in-the-middle</em> is the firm that buys
    neither identity: premium pricing without premium differentiation, or full costs without premium prices.
  </p>
  <p>
    The deeper claim of Porter's 1996 essay "What Is Strategy?" is that a position is defensible only if it is
    a <strong>system of trade-offs</strong>. Southwest's point-to-point, no-meals, no-assigned-seats, single-
    aircraft-type model is hard to copy not because any one choice is secret but because a full-service rival
    copying it must strand its own hubs, crews and frequent-flyer machine — imitation is self-destructive.
    Strategy, in this reading, is the set of things you deliberately refuse to do. The intermediate lessons'
    marketing-mix framework becomes, here, an exercise in coherence: every P either reinforces the position or
    quietly betrays it.
  </p>

  <h2>Moats: the durability question</h2>
  <p>
    A position earns this year's profit; a <strong>moat</strong> earns the next decade's. Buffett's framing
    asks one question: how long can excess returns survive attack? The standard taxonomy has seven
    load-bearing structures. <strong>Network effects</strong>: value grows superlinearly — a network of n users
    has n × (n − 1) / 2 possible connections, so going from 10 to 100 users multiplies potential links 110-fold
    (from 45 to 4,950); winner-take-most dynamics follow. <strong>Switching costs</strong>: an ERP system whose
    replacement costs a year of retraining can sustain margins no product feature could. <strong>Brand</strong>:
    trust that shortens decisions and licenses premium prices. <strong>Cost advantages from scale</strong>:
    unit costs that fall with volume create a moving target no challenger can reach. <strong>Intangible
    assets</strong>: patents and regulatory licences with legal clocks attached. <strong>Efficient
    scale</strong>: markets too small for a second efficient entrant. <strong>Counter-positioning</strong>: the
    incumbent cannot copy the newcomer without destroying its own business model. Note what is <em>not</em> on
    the list: technology alone, being first, or market share. Share in a structurally bad industry (airlines,
    again) is just a bigger share of the losses.
  </p>

  <h2>How strong positions fall: disruption</h2>
  <p>
    Christensen's <em>Innovator's Dilemma</em> (1997) explains the regularity behind the graveyard:
    incumbents are usually defeated from <strong>below</strong>. Entrants launch a product that is worse by the
    incumbents' best customers' criteria but cheaper, simpler, or more convenient — and the incumbents rationally
    ignore it, because their customers do not want it and their margins depend on the existing model. But the
    entrant's trajectory improves along its own axis (steel minimills, personal computers, streaming, digital
    photography) until it meets mainstream demand at a cost structure the incumbent cannot match. The dilemma
    is genuine: the processes and values that make a great company great are exactly what blind it to the low
    end. The strategic countermeasures are structural, not motivational — autonomous units allowed to build
    small-margin businesses, and honest monitoring of the entrants' <em>rate</em> of improvement rather than
    their current adequacy.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked five-forces reading</h2>
  <p>
    The framework earns its keep when it produces a <em>comparative</em> judgement, so run one. Take two
    regional businesses: a coffee shop and an industrial valve distributor. Coffee shop: entry barriers are
    trivial (an espresso machine and a lease), so new-entrant threat scores high; buyers are price-tolerant
    but switching costs are zero and every street has substitutes, so buyer and substitute power score
    high; suppliers (beans, milk) are commodity, so supplier power is low; rivalry is intense. Verdict: a
    structurally hostile industry — margins must come from location, atmosphere and brand, i.e. from
    <em>building</em> the forces the industry lacks. Valve distributor: only a few manufacturers exist
    (supplier power high), but customers — plant operators who spec the valves into million-pound projects
    — face redesign costs and qualification processes to switch (buyer power low, switching costs high);
    entry requires certifications and stock depth; substitutes are rare for a spec'd part. Verdict: a
    structurally attractive niche hiding inside an "unglamorous" industry. The lesson generalises:
    <strong>the forces are a diagnostic instrument, not a slide</strong> — score them, weigh them, and
    they will point to exactly where strategy must work hardest (building switching costs in the coffee
    shop; deepening supplier relationships in the distribution business).
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three refinements separate expert reading from textbook recitation. <strong>Empirical status of the five
    forces</strong>: the framework was induced from industrial-organization economics (Structure-Conduct-
    Performance), and its components — concentration, entry barriers, buyer/supplier concentration — do
    correlate with measured industry profitability, though explaining differences between firms <em>within</em>
    an industry requires the resource-based view (Barney's VRIN tests: valuable, rare, inimitable,
    non-substitutable capabilities). <strong>Porter versus Christensen is partly a timescale argument</strong>:
    five forces describe the present equilibrium; disruption describes how the equilibrium gets overturned.
    Sound strategy runs both analyses and asks which force binds <em>when</em>. <strong>Blue-ocean strategy</strong>
    (Kim and Mauborgne) is Porter with different vocabulary: Cirque du Soleil kept the tent and the clowns,
    deleted the animals and the star performers, and added theatre — value innovation on the eliminate-reduce-
    raise-create grid rather than a fight on the existing value-cost frontier.
  </p>
  <p>
    Open questions are live in strategy precisely because the subject has no laboratory. How durable are network
    effects really — do platforms fall to multi-homing (users keeping two apps) faster than to direct attacks?
    Can incumbents buy disruption (acquiring the entrant) or does the acquisition kill the entrant's model? And
    in the data era, is <em>speed of learning</em> replacing scale as the core moat — a question the
    capability-based literature is only beginning to formalize? What is settled is the discipline this lesson
    ends with: the real strategy of a firm is not its deck but its resource allocation — where the budget, the
    best people, and the CEO's calendar actually go.
  </p>
  <p>
    One practical synthesis turns the lesson into a manager's working method. <strong>Annual strategy review,
    in five moves</strong>: (1) score the five forces for your industry and note their <em>trend</em> (is
    buyer power rising because a comparison platform launched?); (2) name your generic strategy and audit the
    marketing mix for coherence — every activity either reinforces the position or is a candidate for
    deletion; (3) inventory the moats honestly (which of the seven do you actually hold, and is each
    strengthening or eroding?); (4) run a Christensen scan — who could attack from below with a good-enough
    product, and what is their improvement rate?; (5) only then write the plan, as a resource-allocation
    document with owners and dates. Firms that work in this order produce strategy; firms that start at step
    five produce slogans — and the difference surfaces, within a few budget cycles, in exactly the places
    the five forces predicted.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Porter, <em>Competitive Strategy</em> (1980) and "What Is Strategy?" (1996); Christensen, <em>The Innovator's Dilemma</em> (1997); Greenwald &amp; Kahn, <em>Competition Demystified</em>; Barney (1991); Kim &amp; Mauborgne, <em>Blue Ocean Strategy</em> (2005).</p>

